<h1>order-preserving minimal perfect hashing</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="minimalPerfectHash.html"><em>minimal perfect hashing</em></a> function for keys in S such that if k1, k2 &isin; S and k1 &gt; k2, then f(k1) &gt; f(k2).
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="minimalPerfectHash.html"><em>minimal perfect hashing</em></a>, <a href="linearhash.html"><em>linear hash</em></a>, <a href="lasVegas.html"><em>Las Vegas algorithm</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="pearsonshash.html"><em>Pearson's hash</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
For example, if the keys are stored in order in an <a href="array.html"><em>array</em></a>, the array offsets are an order preserving minimal perfect hash of the keys.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorBJ">BJ</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Zbigniew J. Czech, George Havas</strong>, and <strong>Bohdan S. Majewski</strong>, <em>An Optimal Algorithm for Generating Minimal Perfect Hash Functions</em>, Information Processing Letters, 43(5):257-264, October 1992.  Available at DOI <a href="https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.51.5566" target="_blank">10.1.1.51.5566</a>. "It uses expected linear time and ... runs very fast in practice."
</p>

